today I want to talk about something a  bit unusual that was discovered a little  while ago it's called it's got a funny  name it's called Hanny's Voorwerp I may  be pronouncing that completely wrong any  of our Dutch viewers can correct me  this is an object that was discovered by  accident as many of the the most  exciting astronomical discoveries are  made and it was actually discovered by a  member of the public a schoolteacher in  Holland named Hanna van Arkel and so the  name is Hanny's Voorwerp  and Vorwerk I understand means Dutch for  objects so this is Hanna's object and  that's what it looks like it's a bit of  a funny-looking thing so one of the  things that we do as astronomers is we  take pictures it's one of the most basic  things that we can do and even now even  when we have digital images from great  big telescopes we still get a lot of  information by looking at them with our  eye so typical thing for an astronomer  like myself to do would be to cycle  through maybe hundreds or even thousands  of images of individual objects so this  is from my own survey so these are  images from the Hubble Space Telescope  here's a beautiful galaxy that's we're  seeing it's a disc like our own Milky  Way but we're seeing an edge on and  we'll see a huge variety of different  kinds of objects great big round ones  things with spiral arms things that  might call that a train wreck you know  might be the collision of two galaxies  something that's been really disruptive  so even though we have automated  routines to gather some of this  information we can teach a computer to  do this those automated routines are  still not perfect and there's still a  lot to be gained by using our AI brain  system which is really good at picking  out particular patterns or particular  features so in my own research I may  have gone through probably about four or  five thousand of these images recording  whether there have spiral arms whether  they're round whether they have bars  whether they're all messed up and  together with a team of maybe about  seven or eight other people we could  cover in my survey about six or seven  thousand galaxies with each one being  done a couple of times to make sure that  we agree on our classification but what  about when you have a survey like the  Sloan Digital Sky Survey where you  actually have millions of galaxies and  that's far too many galaxies for any  sane individual to look through one by  one so in this case a project called  Galaxy Zoo has enlisted members of the  public to help with this project Galaxy  Zoo is is is a project that I'm not  involved with but is close to our hearts  here in Nottingham because our  collaborator our colleague Steven  Bamford down the hall plays a key role  in this project and they've enlisted  literally hundreds of thousands of  people members of the general public  schoolchildren amateur astronomers  people off the street tell me and me and  Brady of course to help with this with  this problem how many galaxies are you  looked up I did about 20 oh I don't  think you're up there in the top and  this is the Galaxy Zoo webpage at Galaxy  Zoo org so a Galaxy Zoo participant once  they register and log in they do the  same thing they do they are presented  with pictures of galaxies and they look  at them and they're asked a very  specific set of questions or criteria to  really narrow down the focus of the  science and all of those classifications  are combined in a very clever  statistical way and project has shown  that these classifications are as robust  as the classifications of done by  professional astronomers like ourselves  and in the first leg of the project as I  said they had 150,000 people classifying  a million galaxies submitting 50 million  classifications I couldn't speak for all  of them but a lot of it is a real you  get a real hands-on connection with with  the actual science with the images the  images themselves are beautiful you're  looking at spectacular pictures of  galaxies collections of hundreds of  billions of stars billions and billions  of light-years away and then there's  always this the seed this possibility  that you're going to uncover something  that nobody else has  and that's exactly what happened in the  case of Hannah's rule so Hannah van  Arkel the schoolteacher from Holland was  a participant in the Galaxy Zoo project  and she she uncovered this strange  object I think she posted on the Galaxy  Zoo forms to ask if anyone else knew  what this was no one could explain it  and it became an object of interest it  became something out of the ordinary  something unexpected and that's always  what we like to go and follow up so what  you're seeing here is several we'll lots  of galaxies there's this one big galaxy  here which you can see has hints of  spiral arms there's another companion  galaxy next to it but what we're  interested in is this green object here  and this is Hanny's Voorwerp  so it's an irregular shape that already  tells us there's something strange about  this object and in the color scheme that  we are shown here it's bright green so  it's very different in color from any of  the other galaxies around it and that  means it's emitting light at a  particular frequency and at a  particularly narrow frequency so as I  said this was a puzzle it wasn't  immediately obvious what this object was  members of the Galaxy Zoo project and  other astronomers around the world have  since followed it up with many different  telescopes at different wavelengths  trying to find out as much as they could  about it what they've determined is that  it doesn't have any stars of its own  it's mostly gas and dust it's probably  the remnants of a little tiny galaxy  that's been tightly pulled apart by  interacting gravitationally with this  much larger galaxy but we might not  normally see that because the gas or the  dust would within the absence of stars  wouldn't be very bright what they think  has happened is that something has  dumped a lot of energy into this gas its  ionized it and it's made it glow at this  particular narrow wavelength that object  could be something like a quasar so a  supermassive black hole accreting  material and spewing out tons  tons of energy but there's no smoking  gun in this picture at any wavelengths  we can't or they can't actually see the  candidate quasar it may be very very  hidden it may be hidden in the center of  this galaxy here but another possibility  which is very intriguing is that it was  spewing out all this energy but stopped  and we've had enough time for that that  energy to reach Penny's warp ionize that  gas and make it glow but that gap could  be as long as a hundred thousand years  so what we could be seeing here is  what's called a light echo of a quasar  that's now dormant it's now dead but we  can still see what kind of effects its  had on these objects around it
 

